From 4d5c0bf4646a7dbc7f044e8561b3127b38b8e1c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:51:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Less freshly touched memory in the first forward and backward
 call

A first call pays for every page of memory it touches for the first time
(about 1 us per 4 kB page on the Xeon: first call minus warm call is 6 ms for
5500 page faults in the forward pass). Three places touched more than needed:

- The carrier system was built as a dense n x (n + 1) array, copied once more
  and then read back at the pattern positions (twice 1.3 MB for 286 unknowns).
  The pattern, the base values and the slots of the written entries are now
  kept as short arrays; the values go straight into CSC order.
- signal_values scaled the placing rows with three intermediate arrays of the
  size of `pre` (1.6 MB each). The sign now goes on the carrier factor and the
  rows are written in blocks of 128.
- The reverse pass gathered `right[cols]` for all visited entries at once
  (2.1 MB) and formed `signal * alpha` as a whole (1.2 MB). Both products now
  run in blocks of 128 rows, whose intermediate array is reused.

Page faults of the first calls on x86-64 with the native kernel, including
the two earlier commits: forward 5703 -> 4028, backward 1996 -> 1313. All
benchmark outputs and the gradient are bit-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 differometor/__pycache__/fast.cpython-312.pyc | Bin 116565 -> 118152 bytes
 differometor/fast.py                          |  80 ++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 52 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

